---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '@lib/config/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Our Methodology | CalcFalcon"
  description="How CalcFalcon builds, sources, and maintains its calculators — formula derivation, data sourcing, review cadence, and how we correct errors."
>
  <div class="py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4">
      <h1 class="heading-lg mb-8">Our Methodology</h1>

      <div class="prose prose-neutral prose-lg max-w-none">
        <p>
          This page explains how CalcFalcon's calculators are built, where the numbers come from, how often
          we check them, and what happens when we find a mistake. We publish it because financial estimation
          tools are only useful if you can trust the math behind them — and trust starts with showing our
          work.
        </p>

        <h2>How We Build a Calculator</h2>
        <p>
          Every calculator starts from a documented formula, not a guess. For tax calculators, that means
          working from the relevant IRS publication or Revenue Procedure line by line — tax brackets,
          standard deductions, FICA rates, the self-employment tax's 92.35% adjustment, capital gains bracket
          stacking, and Net Investment Income Tax thresholds are all implemented directly from primary IRS
          sources, not third-party summaries. For platform-fee calculators (YouTube AdSense, Etsy, DoorDash,
          Airbnb, and similar), we start from the platform's published fee schedule and terms.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each formula is implemented as a pure, isolated function, separate from the interface that collects
          your inputs. That separation lets us test the math on its own and update a rate or bracket in one
          place without touching how the calculator looks or behaves.
        </p>

        <h2>Where Our Tax Data Comes From</h2>
        <p>
          Every tax and finance calculator that cites IRS figures displays a visible <strong>"Sources &amp;
          Last Updated"</strong> block directly on the calculator page. It names the specific IRS publication,
          Revenue Procedure, or Social Security Administration fact sheet the numbers come from, states which
          tax year the figures apply to, and gives the date we last checked them against that source. If
          you're ever unsure whether a number is current, check that block before you check anything else on
          the page.
        </p>

        <h2>Review Cadence</h2>
        <p>
          Federal tax brackets, standard deductions, and Social Security wage bases are set annually by the
          IRS and SSA, typically announced in the final months of the preceding year. We review our tax
          calculators against the newly published figures each time the IRS releases them and update the
          "Sources &amp; Last Updated" date accordingly.
        </p>
        <p>
          In practice that means an annual pass over every tax calculator once the IRS publishes the new
          year's Revenue Procedure, and a review of platform-fee calculators whenever a platform announces
          a pricing change. Rather than claim a cadence we can't prove, each calculator states the tax year
          its figures cover and the date those figures were last checked, so you can judge for yourself how
          current a given page is. If a calculator's stated tax year is behind the current filing year,
          treat its output as a historical estimate and verify against current IRS figures before relying
          on it.
        </p>

        <h2>How We Correct Errors</h2>
        <p>
          If you find a figure that looks outdated, a formula that looks wrong, or a broken calculator,
          please tell us at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`} class="text-primary-600 hover:underline">{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
          or through our <a href="/contact" class="text-primary-600 hover:underline">contact form</a>. We
          verify the report against the primary source, fix the underlying formula or constant if it's wrong,
          and update the calculator's "Sources &amp; Last Updated" date to reflect the correction. We do not
          silently edit published figures without updating that date — if the numbers on a page changed, the
          date changes with them.
        </p>

        <h2>What Our Calculators Are Not</h2>
        <p>
          Our calculators are general-purpose estimation tools. They cannot account for every detail of your
          individual situation — state-specific rules, prior-year carryovers, credits you may qualify for, or
          circumstances a form field doesn't ask about. Read our full
          <a href="/disclaimer" class="text-primary-600 hover:underline">financial disclaimer</a> for what
          that means before you rely on a result.
        </p>

        <h2>Editorial Independence</h2>
        <p>
          CalcFalcon is supported by display advertising and affiliate partnerships — see
          <a href="/about#how-we-make-money" class="text-primary-600 hover:underline">how we make money</a>
          for the full breakdown. Affiliate relationships never change what a calculator computes. A
          calculator's math is the same whether or not an affiliate link appears next to it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
